      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d be curious to see what json schema\u0027s error messages look like when a password doesn\u0027t match a regex... json schema\u0027s errors are usually super vague, so it might be a question of whether we can provide a better user experience by implementing this (important) bit of validation ourselves.",
      "revId": "72718fe8853924c8b5d461d3047db711641f9f4a",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_31bfd1fd",
        "filename": "keystone/common/validation/validators.py",
        "patchSetId": 37
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 2903
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-10T16:43:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is a regex really the direction we want to go here? I worry it is very prone to failure (regex crafting is flexible but often not easy). Perhaps we should lean on cracklib instead of needing to re-invent this validation?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 57
      },
      "revId": "72718fe8853924c8b5d461d3047db711641f9f4a",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_be06f0ca",
        "filename": "keystone/common/validation/validators.py",
        "patchSetId": 37
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 4
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-16T15:53:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Regular expressions to validate passwords against various standards are well documented and easy to find. Using regex here allows deployers maximum flexibility. That said, we of course have two problems now.",
      "parentUuid": "7aa08908_31bfd1fd",
      "revId": "72718fe8853924c8b5d461d3047db711641f9f4a",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_1396c73d",
        "filename": "keystone/common/validation/validators.py",
        "patchSetId": 37
      },
      "lineNbr": 31,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-13T19:46:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Typically when an system requires you to have a certain level of password strength - it will give you a set of rules. Like: \n\n    - must be at least 8 characters long\n    - must have one capitalized character\n    - must contain one number\n\netc... do we have a way of providing that information here? The user doesn\u0027t have any way to know what things their password is missing when they see this error message.",
      "revId": "72718fe8853924c8b5d461d3047db711641f9f4a",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
